#line(length: 100%)

Let's step out of the official narrative a bit, put our tin foil hats on and look at the wonderful world of possibilities.

The following section here is mainly just going to discuss a hypothesis of sorts.

Is any of it reality?

_*Maybe*, it's grounded in reality._

Is any of it important?

_Only in the same vein as the Rachel Levin credit card._

Hopefully I will spare you - and myself, from making this sound too accusatory but I feel like it's important to always question
things - science rewards curiosity even if it killed the cat.

If you were #link("https://time.com/7302177/epstein-mossad-israel-intelligence-naftali-bennett/")[running a global blackmail ring] for \<insert the #strike[poison] country of your choosing here\>
and wanted to know who was going where, staying at what hotel and meeting with who - like Darkhotel.

You could tail them.

That costs time and capital and is highly prone to human failures.

You could also adopt a well known hacking strategy against a severely vulnerable industry that just so happens to
host elites around the world for everything from a friendly wedding and socializing with foreign women,
to a multi-nation industrial project designed to skirt the geopolitical monopolies of competing nations.

But that comes with a cost.

You can't just hack a single hotel and call it a day.

Besides, most dignitaries or high profile figures travel as secretly as possible and tend to check in under aliases or
names of their staffers to avoid all sorts of problems when they just want to enjoy a beautiful city with their kids -
or praise a transnational railway that benefits them economically.

#aside[
  So, what does a person - _or unit_, do?
]

You hack the vulnerable bits, the places where people are out schmoozing each other, buying drinks or having lunch with each other.

This leaves another question on the table though, how does one go from vulnerable @POS:a to centralized and hardened servers of
a global organization?

Well, you could brute force it for one - but that takes time and is prone to problems of its own right.

You could also use a known variable - like a credit card number, as a beacon to map LAN network topologies.

You could use a business in a chaotic process of actively trying to transition away from a larger corporate entity, where
open gaps in network surveillance and all sorts of credentials get dispensed out to make that transition happen timely.

All while a close personal aide of yours is actively identifying social vulnerabilities in the very personnel managing the
transition and @POS:a equipment on the ground.

You could model the intrusion after an entire existing reality hoping the media, researchers and public embed that reality
into your narrative.

You could use a 5-6 month gap to plan and map the network before running a small trial penetration and then finally
running the real penetration at the right moment during the height of _some conference_ - a transnational chess tournament, and railway construction project
where tensions are high and focus is elsewhere.

I suppose it's also important to note - unlike the 2013 Target Hack and entirely like the Darkhotel hacks, the
credit card information (@PII:a) that got stolen has never been linked to any black market liquidation sites.

#aside[
  If you were cybercriminals looking for credit card information to steal, you most certainly would liquidate your spoils
  as fast as possible - _right_?

  Information like that is only valuable to financial criminals _right_?

  I say that in jest, but there's a bit of snark to it as well.
  That credit card information is most certainly valuable to a financial criminal but it's also valuable to another group of people.
]
